format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

lesson the-dose-scale
title: The dose scale
minutes: 5
xp: 50
teaches: u4-everyone-dose
teaches: u4-dose-steps
relies_on: u2-not-always-dangerous
relies_on: u3-three-units

description:
    Radioactive does not always mean dangerous. You will learn how doses are counted, what an ordinary year adds up to, and where a scan or an illness sits on the scale.

cite ref-epa-radiation-sources

supports:
    The average yearly dose is about 6.2 millisieverts, most of it from natural background radiation; a whole body CT is about 10 millisieverts.

cite ref-who-ionizing-radiation
supports: The dose threshold for acute radiation syndrome is about 1 sievert (1000 millisieverts).

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: How a dose is counted
figure: millisievert-figure
figure_at: 1

body:
    Radiation is all around us, so the question is always how much. A dose measures how much radiation a body has taken in, and it is counted in [[sievert|sieverts]]. Everyday doses are tiny, so they are given in millisieverts: 1 sievert is 1000 [[millisievert|millisieverts]].

card c02
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l1-fill-milli
connected: true

card c03
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: A little, every day
figure: a-year-of-dose
figure_at: 1

body:
    Everyone gets a small dose all the time. In the United States the average is about 6.2 millisieverts a year, and most of it is natural [[background-radiation|background radiation]]. Rocks, soil, space and [[radon|radon]] in the air all add to it.

card c04
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l1-tom-workers
connected: true

card c05
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l1-choice-average

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: From tiny to huge
figure: dose-ladder
figure_at: 1

body:
    Put some doses in a line. Living beside a nuclear power station for a year adds under 0.01 millisieverts. A year of cosmic rays at sea level is about 0.3, and radon in an average home about 2.3. One whole body CT is about 10. About 1000 in a short time is where people start to become ill.

card c07
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l1-order-doses
connected: true

card c08
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l1-choice-ill

card c09
kind: teach
type: explain
tier: deeper
scored: 0
title: Go deeper: where you live
figure: a-year-of-dose
figure_at: 1

body:
    Altitude changes the dose. Living in Denver, about 5,280 feet up, adds about 0.8 millisieverts a year from cosmic radiation, compared with about 0.3 at sea level. Where you live changes your yearly dose a little.
